// Concurrency + GC stress suite — the Bit port of the deleted harness `stress`
// (task #350, ported under #1593's retire-the-seed programme). Each subdirectory of
// `_tests_/stress/` is a Bit program that hammers spawn / channels / select / the
// collector and prints one deterministic line encoding its own correctness. Each
// is compiled, then run TWICE: once under the default collector policy and once
// under `BIT_GC=stress`, which collects at every safepoint. The stress run is a
// precise-rooting oracle — any root the compiler or runtime fails to report is
// swept the instant it stops being marked, so a rooting bug that a rare
// production collection would only occasionally hit becomes a deterministic
// wrong answer here. Both runs must reproduce the program's `.expected` stdout.
//
// THE SEED PASS IS GONE, NOT PORTED, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING TO NOTICE.
// The deleted harness built every program with BOTH compilers — `bit-seed`
// in-process and self-hosted `bit` as a subprocess — and its header carried a
// paragraph (#1413) about how the self-hosted pass being absent had once read as
// coverage. This port drives the SELF-HOSTED COMPILER ONLY. That is deliberate:
// the seed is the retiring bootstrap oracle, `bit` is what ships, and a harness
// written in Bit cannot import a non-Bit module in-process anyway. But #1413's
// lesson runs both ways — a dropped pass that nobody writes down reads as
// coverage exactly like a missing one did. It is dropped, it is stated here, and
// it belongs on #1593's ledger.
//
// THE ORACLE IS ONLY AS DENSE AS THE SAFEPOINTS, and a program must earn it.
// Codegen emits `bit_rt_safepoint` at loop back-edges (runtime/ABI.md §4);
// `bit_rt_alloc` does NOT poll, so allocating does not by itself collect. A
// straight-line program therefore runs ZERO collections under `BIT_GC=stress` no
// matter how much it allocates, and its second run verifies nothing about
// rooting — it is the same run twice. #1438 made that rule enforced rather than
// merely stated: every stress run sets `BIT_GC_STATS=1` and must show a non-zero
// collection count, and a legitimately straight-line program declares itself
// with a non-empty `no-collect` file saying why.
//
// AND THE POLICY IS NOW PROVEN TO HAVE ARRIVED, WHICH #1438's OWN GUARD COULD
// NOT DO. `collections != 0` cannot tell a real `BIT_GC=stress` run from the
// default run repeated — the churn programs collect under the default policy
// too — so a harness whose env plumbing silently failed would pass #1438's check
// on every program while exercising precise rooting exactly zero times. The
// aggregate stats line does not name the active policy (that is the honest fix,
// and it needs `statsReport` in runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/boot.bit to emit
// `policy=<default|stress|off>`), so this port closes the hole differentially
// instead: BOTH runs carry `BIT_GC_STATS=1`, and a MAJORITY of the oracled
// programs must show a stress count more than `stressRatio` times their default
// count. Allocation-driven programs clear it by two to five orders of magnitude
// (`gcgrow`: 1 against 1,476,203). Clock-paced ones do not and must not be made
// to — `schedtimer` measures 99 against 41 — which is why the assertion is over
// the CORPUS, where it belongs: "the environment reached the child" is a fact
// about this harness, not about any one program. A `stress-waived` program gets
// the mirror proof directly: its second run is `BIT_GC=off`, which must collect
// EXACTLY zero.
//
// A MISSING STATS LINE IS FATAL, NEVER A SKIP. `osRunBounded` returns only an
// exit code, so stdout and stderr exist solely as files the wrapper redirected
// into — if the wrapper never execs, or a redirect is dropped, the reads come
// back empty and every field lookup "finds nothing". Treating that as "this
// build does not report the field" would switch the rooting oracle off for the
// whole corpus in one line with no error text anywhere. Absent is an error —
// with one DECLARED exception, `no-stats`, for a program that replaces the
// runtime's own write/exit provider and so never reaches `statsReport`
// (`rootiodarwin`). Declared, with a reason, and it costs that program the
// collection oracles while keeping its stdout comparison under both policies.
//
// A THIRD MARKER, AND IT IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THIS FILE — `stress-waived`.
// `no-collect` says "the collector cannot matter here"; `stress-waived` says
// "the collector DOES matter here and this program does not survive it yet". The
// second run switches to `BIT_GC=off`, so the program keeps proving its stdout
// under one policy and proves nothing about rooting under the other. The
// marker's whole design is that the hole cannot hide: the reason is mandatory,
// and it is printed on EVERY run. A waiver never suppresses the stdout
// comparison — only the policy changes.
//
// EVERY SUBPROCESS CARRIES A WALL-CLOCK DEADLINE. This is the corpus most able
// to hang, and a program that deadlocks must be reported as a failure naming
// itself rather than stalling the suite. `osRunBounded` (ABI.md §19) bounds the
// compile and both runs. `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S` overrides the 300s default.
// CAVEAT, and it is a real divergence from what `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S=0` once
// meant: it used to be "block forever", and `osRunBounded` has no unbounded mode — it
// clamps to 100 minutes. `0` is therefore honored as 100 MINUTES here, loudly, rather
// than silently redefined.
//
// A per-program `timeout-s` file beside `no-collect` (#2695) goes one step
// further: it wins over `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S`, which wins over the 300s default.
//
// A per-program `workers` file beside `timeout-s` (#2583) pins BIT_WORKERS for
// this one program's two runs, ahead of whatever the ambient environment (a
// gate's own env, a developer's shell) already had - `../schedpreempt` is the
// first user: its whole claim is that the scheduler can force ONE worker to
// hand itself back to another ready task, which two or more workers would let
// the peer task satisfy on its own OS thread regardless of whether preemption
// works at all.
//
// THE DEADLINE ITSELF SCALES WITH OBSERVED HOST LOAD (#2401). `defaultTimeoutS`
// is calibrated against an idle host, and a correct program can legitimately
// take several times longer under contention — 485s against the 300s default
// with six agents building, byte-identical to `.expected` when re-run alone.
// `verify.bit`'s `loadProbe`/`runScript` sample load fresh before every bounded
// subprocess and multiply the base deadline accordingly (capped at
// `loadScaleCap`x); at idle-relative load the multiplier is 1 and the wall is
// exactly the constant below. A program whose deadline elapses even after
// scaling is `inconclusive`, not `failed` — see `checkProgram`'s `badVerdict`
// and `main`'s judged/inconclusive summary — because "the deadline could not
// judge this" and "this program is wrong" are different claims, and folding
// them together is what made a busy host indistinguishable from a real hang.
// This does NOT rescue a program whose own baseline (idle) runtime already
// exceeds the wall — that is #2210, a different, already-decided fix.
//
// WHY A `/bin/sh` WRAPPER. `osRunBounded(path, timeout_ms)` takes no argv, no
// environment and captures no output, so a shell script is the only way to hand
// a child `BIT_GC=stress` and keep its stdout. `exec` in the wrapper is
// LOAD-BEARING, not style: without it the shell stays as the child, and
// `osRunBounded`'s SIGKILL on timeout kills the shell and orphans the deadlocked
// stress binary — precisely the hazard this corpus exists to catch. The honest
// fix is an `osRunBoundedEnv(path, argv, env, timeout_ms)` primitive that returns
// captured pipes; until it exists this suite has a shell dependency the
// deleted harness did not have.
//
// Run it: `bit-out/bin/bit run _tests_/bit/stress`, from the repository root.
// `BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR` / `BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY` narrow the run for debugging and
// mutation-testing; either one turns the corpus floors off and prints NOT A GATE.
//
// THIS HARNESS IS A THREE-FILE DIRECTORY MODULE (#2867, widened by #3574),
// split only along existing seams, not by line count. `stress.bit` (this
// file) keeps the corpus census and its rationale, the exclusive/batch
// partition, and `main`. Sibling `verify.bit` keeps the per-program machinery
// `main` calls into: build, run under both GC policies, and compute a
// `Verdict`. Sibling `setup.bit` keeps the string primitives single-file mode
// has no prelude for, plus the target/timeout/private-compiler setup `main`
// performs once before the corpus loop — #3574 split it out of this file to
// clear the 800-line ceiling after formatting. Files side by side in one
// directory share ONE scope — no `import` between them — so every name
// declared in any of the three is visible in the others, exactly as if this
// were still a single file.

// Upper bound on the directory walk (Power of 10 rule 2).
const maxPrograms: int = 256

// `_tests_/stress/parallel/` (#2538/#2539) is a DIFFERENT corpus: standalone
// single-file programs run individually via `bit run <file>.bit`, gated by
// its own `test-stress-parallel` (scripts/stress-parallel.sh), never as one
// directory-per-program legacy entry. This walk requires `<name>.expected`
// per directory and builds the whole directory as ONE module - neither holds
// for `parallel`, which has no aggregate `.expected` and would collide two
// `fn main()` together the moment it holds a second file. #3397.
const excludedDirName: string = "parallel"

// Corpus floors. A suite that is one loop over a directory reports "0 programs,
// 0 failures" and exits 0 when the walk finds nothing, so the counts are
// asserted rather than trusted. `minRunnable` is the per-host half: 44 of the 94
// directories the walk considers (_tests_/stress/*, minus `excludedDirName`) carry
// a host marker, so a gate that skipped everything would still look busy.
//
// THEY ARE THE EXACT COUNTS, not a comfortable margin, and that is what makes
// adding a program bump them. Left slack they drift: these read 79/60 while the
// corpus already held 80 directories, so one addition had gone unrecorded and a
// second could have too.
//
// #2582 added `../schedpreempt` (no host marker, so it counts on both sides).
// Its branch was cut when the corpus held 89 walk directories and predicted
// 90/69; three more landed while it waited, so the numbers below are counted
// off disk at merge time rather than taken from either side of the conflict:
// `ls -d _tests_/stress/*/ | grep -v /parallel/` is 94, and
// `ls _tests_/stress/*/linux-only` is 22, so macOS runs 94 - 22 = 72.
const minPrograms: int = 94
const minRunnable: int = 72

// How many directories must be skipped for belonging to the OTHER host. #2167's
// `spawnjoinregionslinux` is linux-only with no Darwin twin, and the two sides
// move independently: `darwin-only` reached 22 with #2593's
// ./schedgrowdemanddarwin (after #2645's ./stwownstack — a raw OS thread with
// no green task, which needs a real platform thread door — had taken it to
// 21), and `linux-only` reached 22 separately with #3476's ./schedgrowlinux
// (after #3291's ../netclockmono had taken it to 21).
//
// The two sides are EQUAL right now, 22 and 22, but that is a coincidence of
// today's corpus, not something either side owes the other: they were 21/22 as
// recently as #3476, and the next host-only test added to just one side (the
// common case — additions are rarely paired) breaks the equality again, in
// either direction. `minHostSkips` is deliberately a FLOOR well below both
// sides rather than either side's exact value or their shared value: the check
// on each platform reads the OTHER side's skip count (a macOS run's floor is
// really a bound on the linux-only count, a Linux run's on the darwin-only
// count), so the guard is only as safe as the SMALLER of the two — and pinning
// it to today's number assumes that relationship holds, which the 21/22
// history above shows it does not.
// Inverting the host gate still leaves `minRunnable` satisfied; mutation-testing
// found that.
const minHostSkips: int = 19

// How far above its default-policy count a program's stress-policy count must
// sit before the second run counts as having had a different policy. See the
// header: the measured margins are 250x and up.
const stressRatio: int = 8

// The corpus-wide plumbing assertion: at least 1-in-`minProofShare` of the
// oracled programs must clear `stressRatio`, and the check only applies once
// `minProofSample` of them have run (a one-program debugging run proves nothing
// about plumbing and should not pretend to).
const minProofShare: int = 2
const minProofSample: int = 4

const defaultTimeoutS: int = 300
const clampTimeoutMs: int = 6000000

// `timeoutSOverrideMs`'s two non-value results: no file at all, versus a file
// that exists but is not a positive integer. Kept distinct because the two
// cases have opposite handling in the caller - absent falls through to the
// existing bound, invalid fails the one program outright.
const timeoutSAbsent: int = -1
const timeoutSInvalid: int = -2

// `workersOverride`'s two non-value results (#2583), same shape and same
// reason as the pair above: absent lets the ambient BIT_WORKERS (if any)
// stand, invalid fails the one program rather than silently keeping
// whatever the environment already had.
const workersAbsent: int = -1
const workersInvalid: int = -2

const targetX64Linux: int = 0
const targetArm64Linux: int = 1
const targetArm64Macos: int = 2

const aggregatePrefix: string = "[bit-gc] collections="

// --- the exclusive/batch split (#2564) --------------------------------------
//
// `test-stress` used to run every eligible program itself, `exclusive: true`
// (tools/build/gates.bit), because a program that asserts on real elapsed time
// gives a false red when a neighbour gate is co-scheduled on the same cores
// (#2400 was a 4% margin). But most of the corpus asserts only on correctness —
// an exit predicate, a byte comparison, a deterministic counter — and does not
// care who else is on the box.
//
// EXACTLY FOUR NAMES HERE, and each earned its place by actually reading a real
// clock as the DISCRIMINATOR between a pass and a fail, not merely as an
// anti-hang backstop around a boolean/count outcome (`threadJoin`'s deadline,
// the pool families' generous multi-second polls, `chanrace*`'s progress-reset
// stall detector — all of those get SAFER as the box gets slower, since a
// ceiling-only backstop just gets more room). These four do not:
//
//   - `parkdarwin`/`parklinux` drive `park/props`'s P1/P2/P4, which discriminate
//     "really blocked" from "returned immediately" by a *3x* margin (a 150ms
//     floor against a 50ms ceiling) — the tightest window in this corpus, and
//     the shape #2400 broke on elsewhere.
//   - `threaddarwin`/`threadlinux` drive `thread/props`'s P4/P5, a wider 25x-50x
//     margin (40ms/2s, 200ms/5s) but still a genuine elapsed-time discriminator
//     rather than a backstop. Kept in rather than argued out of it: a program
//     whose classification is a judgment call stays on the safe side of it.
//
// Every other real-clock use in the corpus was read and is a backstop, not a
// discriminator (see #2564's ticket for the file-by-file account). Nothing here
// classifies by NAME PATTERN — "thread" and "threaddarwin" share a prefix and
// opposite classifications, because only one of them reads a real clock.
fn exclusiveNames(): []string {
  return ["parkdarwin", "parklinux", "threaddarwin", "threadlinux"]
}

fn isExclusiveProgram(name: string): bool {
  for n of exclusiveNames() {
    if (n == name) {
      return true
    }
  }
  return false
}

// Host-invariant like `minHostSkips`: the four names above are two darwin/linux
// pairs, so the host gate always admits exactly one of each pair, and exactly
// two of the four are ever eligible on one host. An exact count, not a floor,
// for the reason `minRunnable`'s header gives — a name added to one partition
// and not this list would otherwise drift in silence.
const minExclusiveRunnable: int = 2

// The harness-wide context every setup and per-program function threads
// through. `Text`/`Marker`/`Outcome`/`Verdict` — the per-program result types
// `checkProgram` and its callees use — live in `verify.bit` beside the code
// that produces them.
class Ctx {
  root: string,
  compiler: string,
  stdlib: string,
  libbitrt: string,
  scratch: string,
  timeoutMs: int,
  target: int,
}

fn say(s: string) {
  print(s)
  print("\n")
}

// Single-file mode has no prelude, so `string(i)` is not a conversion; the one
// int-to-string road is interpolation.
fn num(v: int): string {
  return "${v}"
}

fn die(s: string) {
  say("stress: FATAL: " + s)
  osExit(1)
}

fn main() {
  let root = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_ROOT"))
  if (len(root) == 0) {
    root = "."
  }
  if (!fsIsDir(root + "/_tests_/stress") || !fsIsDir(root + "/stdlib")) {
    die(
        "run this from the repository root (or set BIT_STRESSGATE_ROOT): '" +
        root +
        "' has no _tests_/stress + stdlib",
    )
  }

  // The two debugging knobs, and the asymmetry between them is deliberate.
  // `BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY` narrows which programs are EXECUTED; it does not touch
  // enumeration or the host gate, so BOTH corpus floors still apply under it and
  // a filtered run cannot hide an empty walk or an inverted host gate.
  // `BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR` replaces the corpus outright, which the floors are
  // counting - so that one, and only that one, turns them off and says so.
  let corpus = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR"))
  let only = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_ONLY"))
  let fixture = len(corpus) > 0
  let partial = fixture || len(only) > 0
  if (len(corpus) == 0) {
    corpus = root + "/_tests_/stress"
  }
  if (partial) {
    say("stress: *** PARTIAL RUN - NOT A GATE *** (BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR/ONLY set)")
  }
  if (fixture) {
    say("stress: corpus floors are OFF - BIT_STRESSGATE_DIR replaced the corpus")
  }

  // `BIT_STRESSGATE_PARTITION` is NOT a debugging knob like the two above: it is
  // how `./make test-stress-exclusive` / `./make test-stress-batch`
  // (tools/build/gates.bit) split one permanent gate into two, so unlike `only`
  // it must NOT print "NOT A GATE" or otherwise read as a lesser run. Unset (the
  // default, and what plain `./make test-stress` uses) runs everything, exactly
  // as before the split existed.
  let partition = trim(osEnv("BIT_STRESSGATE_PARTITION"))
  if (partition != "" && partition != "exclusive" && partition != "batch") {
    die("BIT_STRESSGATE_PARTITION must be 'exclusive', 'batch', or unset - got '" + partition + "'")
  }
  if (partition != "") {
    say("stress: partition '" + partition + "' (BIT_STRESSGATE_PARTITION)")
  }

  let target = hostTarget()
  let compilerSrc = root + "/bit-out/bin/bit"
  let libbitrt = root + "/bit-out/lib/" + targetTriple(target) + "/libbitrt.a"
  if (!fsExists(compilerSrc)) {
    die("no self-hosted compiler at " + compilerSrc)
  }
  if (!fsExists(libbitrt)) {
    die("no runtime archive at " + libbitrt)
  }

  let scratch = "/tmp/bit-stressgate-" + num(timeUnixNs())
  if (fsMkdir(scratch) != 0) {
    die("cannot create scratch dir " + scratch)
  }

  let cx = Ctx{
    root: root,
    compiler: compilerSrc,
    stdlib: root + "/stdlib",
    libbitrt: libbitrt,
    scratch: scratch,
    timeoutMs: timeoutMs(),
    target: target,
  }
  cx = Ctx{
    root: cx.root,
    compiler: privateCompiler(cx, compilerSrc),
    stdlib: cx.stdlib,
    libbitrt: cx.libbitrt,
    scratch: cx.scratch,
    timeoutMs: cx.timeoutMs,
    target: cx.target,
  }

  let names = sortNames(splitNul(fsListDir(corpus)))
  if (len(names) > maxPrograms) {
    die("more than " + num(maxPrograms) + " entries in " + corpus)
  }

  let dirs = []string(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(names) && i < maxPrograms) {
    if (names[i] != excludedDirName && fsIsDir(corpus + "/" + names[i])) {
      dirs = append(dirs, names[i])
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  if (!fixture && len(dirs) < minPrograms) {
    die(
        "found " +
        num(len(dirs)) +
        " program directories in " +
        corpus +
        ", expected at least " +
        num(minPrograms) +
        " - the corpus walk found nothing to run",
    )
  }

  // Apply the host gate as its OWN pass, and check its floor before a single
  // program is built. A program that can only run on one OS marks itself:
  // `darwin-only` is `extern function` (SPEC §11.7) — Bit's ELF output is fully
  // static with no dynamic symbol table, so an extern symbol has nothing to
  // resolve against and the compiler rejects it outright — and `linux-only` is
  // the mirror, for `syscall` (§11.8). Neither compiles elsewhere, so it is a
  // skip, not a failure.
  //
  // Separated from the run loop for two reasons. The floor below is the guard
  // against an INVERTED gate, and an inverted gate selects exactly the programs
  // that cannot compile here — so checking it afterwards means sitting through
  // 19 compile failures to be told the gate is backwards. And `eligible` is
  // counted before `ONLY` is applied, so a filtered run still enforces it: a
  // guard a debugging knob can switch off is not a guard.
  let eligible = []string(0)
  let skippedDarwin = 0
  let skippedLinux = 0
  let e = 0
  while (e < len(dirs)) {
    let dir = corpus + "/" + dirs[e]
    if (fsExists(dir + "/darwin-only") && target != targetArm64Macos) {
      skippedDarwin = skippedDarwin + 1
    } else if (fsExists(dir + "/linux-only") && target == targetArm64Macos) {
      skippedLinux = skippedLinux + 1
    } else {
      eligible = append(eligible, dirs[e])
    }
    e = e + 1
  }
  let skipped = skippedDarwin + skippedLinux
  if (!fixture && len(eligible) < minRunnable) {
    die(
        "only " +
        num(len(eligible)) +
        " of " +
        num(len(dirs)) +
        " programs are runnable on this host (" +
        num(skipped) +
        " skipped), expected at least " +
        num(minRunnable),
    )
  }
  // The count above does NOT catch an inverted gate, and finding that out is the
  // reason this check exists. The corpus carries 19 `darwin-only` and 19
  // `linux-only` directories — inverting the comparison SWAPS which 19 are
  // skipped and leaves the runnable total at 59, green, with a skip count that
  // still looks right. Only counting the two marker kinds SEPARATELY breaks that
  // symmetry: on macOS the darwin-only skip count must be zero and the
  // linux-only one must not be, and inverting the gate reverses exactly that.
  let wrongWay = skippedDarwin
  let rightWay = skippedLinux
  if (target != targetArm64Macos) {
    wrongWay = skippedLinux
    rightWay = skippedDarwin
  }
  if (!fixture && (wrongWay != 0 || rightWay < minHostSkips)) {
    die(
        "host gate is inverted: skipped " +
        num(wrongWay) +
        " programs marked for THIS host and " +
        num(rightWay) +
        " marked for the other (expected 0 and at least " +
        num(minHostSkips) +
        ")",
    )
  }

  let ran = 0
  let failures = 0
  // #2401: a program the (load-scaled) deadline could not judge at all is
  // counted separately from a program that ran and was wrong. `judged` is the
  // corpus-wide denominator this buys - see the summary at the end of `main`.
  let inconclusive = 0
  let oracled = 0
  let proofs = 0

  // The `batch` partition is fanned out through `fanout.bit`'s `runBatch`
  // (#3776) instead of this loop: one `gatefanout` batch (stage every
  // program's jobs, ONE outer `osRunBounded` call, grade in order) rather
  // than `checkProgram` one program at a time. `exclusive` and the plain
  // unpartitioned run still take the loop below unchanged — see fanout.bit's
  // header for why the four timing-sensitive `exclusive` programs cannot
  // share cores with anything else, including each other.
  if (partition == "batch") {
    let batchList = batchNames(eligible, only)
    let batch = runBatch(cx, corpus, batchList)
    ran = batch.ran
    failures = batch.failures
    inconclusive = batch.inconclusive
    oracled = batch.oracled
    proofs = batch.proofs
  } else {
    let k = 0
    while (k < len(eligible)) {
      let name = eligible[k]
      k = k + 1
      if (len(only) > 0 && indexOfAt("," + only + ",", "," + name + ",", 0) < 0) {
        continue
      }
      if (partition == "exclusive" && !isExclusiveProgram(name)) {
        continue
      }
      ran = ran + 1
      let dir = corpus + "/" + name
      // Per-program deadline override (see the header and `resolveTimeoutMs`):
      // an invalid `timeout-s` file fails this one program without touching
      // any other.
      let effTimeoutMs = resolveTimeoutMs(cx, dir)
      if (effTimeoutMs == timeoutSInvalid) {
        say("stress '" + name + "': bad timeout-s file")
        failures = failures + 1
        continue
      }
      // Applied as its own `Ctx` copy for just this call - every other field,
      // and every other program's `cx`, is untouched.
      let pcx = Ctx{
        root: cx.root,
        compiler: cx.compiler,
        stdlib: cx.stdlib,
        libbitrt: cx.libbitrt,
        scratch: cx.scratch,
        timeoutMs: effTimeoutMs,
        target: cx.target,
      }
      let v = checkProgram(pcx, name, dir)
      failures = failures + v.failed
      inconclusive = inconclusive + v.inconclusive
      if (v.oracled) {
        oracled = oracled + 1
      }
      if (v.proved) {
        proofs = proofs + 1
      }
    }
  }

  // A SPLIT THAT SILENTLY DROPS A PROGRAM IS WORSE THAN NO SPLIT: `batch` is
  // defined as "not exclusive", so between the two partitions every eligible
  // name runs exactly once by construction, with no third bucket a typo could
  // fall into - but a typo in `exclusiveNames()` that matches no real directory
  // would move a program from `exclusive` to `batch` without failing anything,
  // which is a real classification bug even though it drops no coverage. This
  // is what catches THAT: an exact count, like `minRunnable`'s own reasoning,
  // and skipped only when a debugging knob has already made `ran` mean
  // something else.
  if (!partial) {
    if (partition == "exclusive" && ran != minExclusiveRunnable) {
      die(
          "stress: partition 'exclusive' ran " +
          num(ran) +
          " programs, expected exactly " +
          num(minExclusiveRunnable) +
          " - exclusiveNames() and the corpus have drifted apart",
      )
    }
    if (partition == "batch" && ran != len(eligible) - minExclusiveRunnable) {
      die(
          "stress: partition 'batch' ran " +
          num(ran) +
          " programs, expected exactly " +
          num(len(eligible) - minExclusiveRunnable) +
          " - exclusiveNames() and the corpus have drifted apart",
      )
    }
  }

  // THE PLUMBING ASSERTION, and it is the one guard the deleted harness could not
  // have. `collections != 0` cannot tell `BIT_GC=stress` from the default run
  // repeated, so if the second wrapper's environment silently stopped arriving
  // every program would still pass its own oracle while precise rooting was
  // never exercised. Collecting at every safepoint costs two to five orders of
  // magnitude, so a majority of the oracled programs must show it. Asserted over
  // the corpus rather than per program because a clock-paced program legitimately
  // cannot clear the margin (see `checkProgram`), and asserted as a SHARE rather
  // than a count so a filtered run enforces it too.
  say(
      "stress: policy proofs " +
      num(proofs) +
      "/" +
      num(oracled) +
      " (stress count above " +
      num(stressRatio) +
      "x the default count)",
  )
  if (oracled >= minProofSample && proofs * minProofShare < oracled) {
    say("stress: FAIL - only " + num(proofs) + " of " + num(oracled) + " oracled programs collected")
    say("  meaningfully more under BIT_GC=stress than under the default policy. That is not a")
    say("  stress pass, it is the default run performed twice: the environment in the second")
    say("  wrapper is not reaching the child.")
    failures = failures + 1
  }

  // #2401's denominator, printed on EVERY run, success or failure: a run that
  // judged nothing must look different from one that judged everything. `ran`
  // is programs that reached `checkProgram`; `judged` is the ones a verdict was
  // actually reached for - the two are equal unless the load-scaled deadline
  // still elapsed somewhere.
  let judged = ran - inconclusive
  say(
      "stress: " +
      num(judged) +
      "/" +
      num(ran) +
      " program(s) judged (" +
      num(inconclusive) +
      " inconclusive)",
  )

  if (inconclusive != 0) {
    // An unjudged program is never a silent pass - the corpus-wide plumbing
    // proof above and the per-program oracle both need every program to have
    // actually run. But it is also not the same CLAIM as `failures`: this is
    // "the deadline elapsed even after scaling for observed load", not "the
    // program produced a wrong answer or would not build" - see runScript's own
    // TIMED OUT line, above, for the load average and scale at each occurrence.
    say(
        "stress: INCONCLUSIVE - " +
        num(inconclusive) +
        " of " +
        num(ran) +
        " program(s) did not finish within the load-scaled deadline. Host contention or a",
    )
    say("  genuine hang - re-run the named program(s) standalone on a quiet host to tell them apart.")
  }

  if (failures != 0 || inconclusive != 0) {
    say(
        "stress: FAIL - " +
        num(ran) +
        " programs run, " +
        num(skipped) +
        " skipped, " +
        num(failures) +
        " failed, " +
        num(inconclusive) +
        " inconclusive",
    )
    osExit(1)
  }
  let tag = "PASS"
  if (partial) {
    tag = "PASS (PARTIAL RUN - NOT A GATE)"
  }
  say(
      "stress: " +
      tag +
      " - " +
      num(ran) +
      " programs run, " +
      num(skipped) +
      " skipped, " +
      num(judged) +
      "/" +
      num(ran) +
      " judged",
  )
}
